==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[File:Calculator.kodabar.jpg|thumb|An electronic calculator]]

===Etymology===
In the sense of a person, from {{inh|en|enm|calkelatour|t=mathematician, astrologer}}, borrowed from {{der|en|la|calculātor}}, equivalent to {{af|en|calculate|-or|id2=agent noun}}.  The other meanings arose in Modern English.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈkæl.kjə.leɪ.tə(ɹ)/}}
* {{IPA|en|/kæl.kjə.leɪ.tɚ/|[ˈkʰæɫ.kjəˌleɪ̯.ɾɚ]|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-calculator.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[mechanical]] or [[electronic]] [[device]] that [[perform]]s [[mathematical]] [[calculation]]s; sometimes, an electronic one specifically.
#: {{hyper|en|device#Noun|machine#Noun}}
#: {{hypo|en|adding machine<qq:sometimes construed as coordinate>}}
#: {{cot|en|abacus}}
#: {{collocation|en|electronic '''calculator'''; &emsp; pocket '''calculator'''}}
#: {{ux|en|The '''calculator''' on a bookkeeper's desk in the 1950s was an adding machine with mechanical guts.}}
#: {{ux|en|I told him to hand me a '''calculator''' and he jokingly placed an adding machine at my side.}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} A person who performs mathematical [[calculation]].
#: {{syn|en|computer<qq:archaic>|reckoner<qq:archaic>}}
#: {{ux|en|During the war, his grandmother had worked at the Bletchley Park decoding station as a '''calculator'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020|author=Paul Krugman|title=Arguing with Zombies: Economics, Politics, and the Fight for a Better Future|page=145|passage=First, many real-world investors bear little resemblance to the cool '''calculators''' of efficient-market theory: they're all too subject to herd behavior, to bouts of irrational exuberance and unwarranted panic.}}
# A person who [[calculate]]s (in the sense of scheming).
#: {{nearsyn|en|schemer|plotter|;|manipulator}}
#: {{ux|en|He revealed himself to be a cold-blooded '''calculator''' out to sabotage his colleagues.}}
#* {{RQ:Bulwer-Lytton Alice|volume=I|chapter=X|page=92|passage=Talk not thus, I implore you, Evelyn: do not imagine me the worldly '''calculator''' that my enemies deem me.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1858|author=John Cumming|title=Thy Word is Truth: an apology for Christianity|page=112|passage=You have in the merchant the shrewd '''calculator''' of probable contingencies; we shall see that we have in the prophet the absolute proclaimer of necessary and inevitable facts.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[set]] of mathematical [[table]]s.
#: {{syn|en|ready reckoner}}

